  "rental-property-tax-deductions-cincinnati": `
<p>Owning a rental property in Cincinnati comes with a meaningful set of tax advantages that many landlords underutilize — either because they don't know they exist or because they don't have the documentation to claim them. Here's a plain-language overview of the major deductions available to Ohio rental property owners.</p>

<h2>Can I deduct mortgage interest on my Cincinnati rental property?</h2>
<p>Yes — mortgage interest on a loan used to acquire or improve a rental property is fully deductible as a rental expense. This is often the largest single deduction for leveraged rental property owners; if your rental carries a $1,500/month mortgage payment and $1,000 of that is interest, that $12,000/year in interest is deductible against your rental income.</p>

<h2>How does depreciation work as a tax deduction for Cincinnati rental owners?</h2>
<p>Depreciation is one of the most powerful tax benefits of rental property ownership — the IRS allows you to deduct the cost of the property (excluding land) over 27.5 years for residential rental property. On a $200,000 building value (excluding land), that's approximately $7,270/year in depreciation deduction regardless of whether you actually spent any money on the property that year. This non-cash deduction often allows rental property owners to show a tax loss even when the property generates positive cash flow.</p>

<h2>What property taxes can Cincinnati rental owners deduct?</h2>
<p>Property taxes paid on your rental property are fully deductible as a rental expense in the year they're paid — for Cincinnati and surrounding Hamilton County properties, this means your real estate taxes paid to the county are deductible against rental income. This is a separate deduction from the capped SALT deduction on your personal return, because rental property taxes are claimed as a business expense on Schedule E.</p>

<h2>Is property management fees tax deductible for Cincinnati rental owners?</h2>
<p>Yes — property management fees paid to EquityTeam or any other professional manager are fully deductible as ordinary and necessary rental expenses. This is one of the most commonly overlooked benefits of professional management: the fee is deductible, making the real after-tax cost meaningfully lower than the face amount.</p>

<h2>What insurance premiums can Cincinnati landlords deduct?</h2>
<p>Premiums paid for landlord insurance (dwelling fire policy), liability coverage, and any other insurance policies specifically covering the rental property are deductible in the year paid. This includes umbrella liability policy premiums allocated to the rental property.</p>

<h2>What repairs and maintenance costs are deductible for rental properties?</h2>
<p>Repairs that maintain the property in its current condition — fixing a leaking pipe, repainting, replacing a broken window — are deductible in the year the expense is incurred. Improvements that add value or extend the property's life (a new roof, an addition, a full kitchen remodel) must be capitalized and depreciated over time rather than expensed immediately. The repair vs. improvement distinction matters and should be reviewed with your CPA.</p>

<h2>Are there other commonly overlooked deductions for Cincinnati rental owners?</h2>
<p>Yes — deductible expenses also include: advertising and marketing costs for finding tenants, professional fees (attorney and CPA fees related to the rental), home office deduction if you have a dedicated space used exclusively for managing your rental business, auto mileage for trips to the property for management and maintenance, and losses from casualty events not covered by insurance. Keep records of all of these throughout the year.</p>
<p>EquityTeam provides detailed monthly statements and year-end financial summaries that make claiming these deductions straightforward. Contact us to learn more about our full-service property management, including financial reporting.</p>
<p><em>This article is provided for general informational purposes and is not tax advice. Consult a CPA or tax professional about your specific situation.</em></p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<p><strong>What is the passive activity loss rule and how does it affect Cincinnati rental property owners?</strong><br>Rental income and losses are generally classified as "passive" by the IRS. If your rental shows a tax loss (common due to depreciation), you can typically only offset that loss against other passive income — not ordinary wages. However, if your adjusted gross income is below $100,000 and you actively participate in managing the rental, you may be able to deduct up to $25,000 in rental losses against ordinary income. A CPA can help you navigate these rules for your specific situation.</p>
<p><strong>What is a 1031 exchange and how can it benefit Cincinnati rental property investors?</strong><br>A 1031 exchange allows you to sell a rental property and defer the capital gains tax by reinvesting the proceeds into a "like-kind" replacement property within specific time limits. Properly executed, a 1031 exchange lets you continue building your Cincinnati or Dayton rental portfolio without paying tax on each sale. This is a complex transaction that requires a qualified intermediary and careful timing — consult a tax professional before initiating a sale you intend to exchange.</p>
<p><strong>Should Cincinnati rental property owners set up an LLC?</strong><br>An LLC can provide liability protection by separating your rental property from your personal assets, though it doesn't change the tax treatment of rental income in most cases. Whether an LLC makes sense depends on your portfolio size, risk exposure, and state tax implications. Ohio has an annual LLC fee — consult both a CPA and a real estate attorney to evaluate whether the protection is worth the cost for your situation.</p>

<p><strong>Related articles:</strong> <a href="real-estate-investing-cincinnati">Reasons to Invest in Cincinnati Real Estate</a> · <a href="renting-vs-selling-cincinnati">Renting vs Selling in Cincinnati</a> · <a href="financing-cincinnati-rental-property">Financing Your Cincinnati Rental</a></p>
`,

